# Project Rules

- SquareClient is the shared Square billing library for the zyzyva fleet — a flexible Elixir client for Square API integration, published for GitHub-based consumption (`{:square_client, github: "zyzyva/square_client"}`). It is a library, not a standalone app.
- It is consumed by every app in the fleet that bills through Square. A change to its public API is a breaking change for every consumer, not just this repo: bump the version in `mix.exs`, record it in `CHANGELOG.md`, and call it out explicitly in the handoff. Precedent: predicate functions were renamed 2026-07-10 (`is_premium_tier?` → `premium_tier?` etc.) and communicated exactly this way.
- Elixir ~> 1.15 (dev/test runs on 1.19). No Phoenix dependency of its own — Ecto and Plug are optional deps. LiveView/Phoenix integration ships as generated code (`priv/templates/*.eex`) that consuming apps opt into via `mix square.*` tasks, not as compiled code this library ships directly.
- Prefer the native `JSON` module (Elixir 1.18+) over Jason for new code, per fleet convention. A couple of older call sites (`lib/square_client/plans.ex`, `lib/mix/tasks/square_client.gen.auth_tests.ex`) still use Jason — leave them alone unless a slice specifically targets them.
- The plan catalog is the single source of truth for plan names, prices, limits, and Square variation ids: `priv/square_plans.json` plus its reader (`lib/square_client/plans.ex`, `lib/square_client/catalog/`) and the `mix square.*` management tasks. Never hardcode a plan name, price, or variation id in a slice — extend the catalog and its reader instead.
- Webhook signature verification (`lib/square_client/webhooks.ex`, `webhook_plug.ex`, `webhook_handler.ex`) is security-critical: it verifies Square's v2 signature scheme over `notification_url <> body`. Any change to this path must keep verification correct, stay covered by tests, and never weaken or bypass it for convenience or debugging.
- Code generator templates under `priv/templates/*.eex` are copied verbatim into consuming apps by `mix square.*` tasks. A slice touching a template must be validated against what it actually renders, not just that the library itself compiles.
- No live Square account or network calls in tests. All HTTP is mocked via `Bypass` and `Mox` (see existing tests for the pattern). Tests must stay fast and hermetic.
- The quality gate is `mix check` (format --check-formatted, credo --strict, compile --warnings-as-errors, test) for a slice in flight, and `mix check.all` (adds dialyzer) before a slice is considered mergeable.
- This library has no running app of its own to demo — verification is the test suite, not browser QA. There is no QA role in this swarm.
